.TH ALIAS 1 "OShell Built-in Commands"
.SH NAME
alias \- handle command aliases
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B alias
[
.I "NAME[='VALUE'] ..."
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B alias
command manages command aliases in OShell. Aliases allow creating shortcuts for commands.
.PP
With no arguments,
.B alias
prints all defined aliases in the format NAME='VALUE'.
.PP
With name arguments only,
.B alias
prints the specified aliases.
.PP
With assignment syntax (NAME='VALUE'),
.B alias
creates or updates aliases.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B NAME
The name of an alias to display (without =VALUE).
.TP
.B NAME='VALUE'
Creates or updates an alias. The value can be quoted with single quotes.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B alias
List all aliases:
.RS
.IP
ll='ls -la'
.br
myls='ls -la *.c'
.RE
.TP
.B alias ll
Display the ll alias:
.RS
.IP
ll='ls -la'
.RE
.TP
.B alias ll='ls -la'
Create alias ll for 'ls -la'.
.TP
.B alias myls='ls -la *.c' mycp='cp -i'
Create multiple aliases.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success.
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
Aliases are only available in the current shell session
.IP \(bu 2
Maximum of 100 aliases can be defined
.IP \(bu 2
If an alias name already exists, its value is replaced
.IP \(bu 2
Single quotes around values are optional but recommended for multi-word values
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bash (1)